# The Peano natural numbers as a self-type, with addition defined by structural recursion, and arithmetic facts PROVED
# BY THE KERNEL (judge.ts) computing normal forms. Unlike the integer-literal facts elsewhere (checked by the decision
# procedures), these reduce `plus one one` to `two` through the derived computing eliminator and the termination-gated
# transparent definition of `plus`, then `calm` (definitional equality / reflexivity) discharges the equation. This is
# the dependent-proof path: a proof is the kernel checking two terms share a normal form. See
# note/library/seed/proof-checking/05-recommendation.md and 09-math-dsl-cheatsheet.md.

# A natural number: zero, or the successor of a natural.
form natural
  case zero
  case succ
    link prior, like natural

# Addition by recursion on the first argument: 0 + b = b, and (succ a) + b = succ (a + b). The recursion descends on
# the matched predecessor, so it is verified terminating and registered transparent (the kernel unfolds it).
task plus
  take a, like natural
  take b, like natural
  like natural
  fork case, read a
    case zero
      send back
        read b
    case succ
      send back
        make succ
          bind prior
            call plus
              read prior
              read b

# The small numerals, as successor towers.
task one
  like natural
  send back
    make succ
      bind prior
        make zero

task two
  like natural
  send back
    make succ
      bind prior
        make succ
          bind prior
            make zero

task three
  like natural
  send back
    make succ
      bind prior
        make succ
          bind prior
            make succ
              bind prior
                make zero

# one plus one is two: the kernel reduces plus one one through the eliminator and recursion, and checks it equals two.
rule one-plus-one-is-two
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call plus
        call one
        call one
      call two
  calm hold

# two plus one is three.
rule two-plus-one-is-three
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call plus
        call two
        call one
      call three
  calm hold

# one plus two is three: addition reduces on the FIRST argument, so this exercises a different reduction path than
# two-plus-one, and still lands on three.
rule one-plus-two-is-three
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call plus
        call one
        call two
      call three
  calm hold

# zero is a left identity, concretely: zero plus three is three (the base case of the recursion).
rule zero-plus-three-is-three
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call plus
        make zero
        call three
      call three
  calm hold

# UNIVERSAL laws proved through the kernel, not just concrete instances. These are definitional: each side reduces to
# the same normal form FOR EVERY value of the bound variables, so `calm` discharges them. Genuine forall-theorems.

# Zero is a left identity for addition: 0 + n = n, for every natural n. (The zero branch of plus returns its second
# argument directly, so plus zero n reduces to n even when n is an arbitrary variable.)
rule plus-zero-left-is-identity
  seat n, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call plus
        make zero
        read n
      read n
  calm hold

# The successor steps out of a sum: (succ a) + b = succ (a + b), for every a and b. (This is the recursion equation of
# plus, definitionally true.)
rule plus-succ-steps-out
  seat a, like natural
  seat b, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call plus
        make succ
          bind prior
            read a
        read b
      make succ
        bind prior
          call plus
            read a
            read b
  calm hold

# Zero is ALSO a right identity: n + 0 = n, for every n. This is NOT definitional (plus recurses on its first argument,
# so plus n zero is stuck on a variable n), and is proved by structural induction on n: the base n = 0 holds by
# computation, and the successor case closes using the induction hypothesis. This is the type's eliminator at work.
rule plus-zero-right-is-identity
  seat n, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call plus
        read n
        make zero
      read n
  fold n

# The successor steps out on the RIGHT: a + (succ b) = succ (a + b), for every a and b. Proved by induction on a (each
# side recurses on a, and the hypothesis aligns the successor cases).
rule plus-succ-right-steps-out
  seat a, like natural
  seat b, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call plus
        read a
        make succ
          bind prior
            read b
      make succ
        bind prior
          call plus
            read a
            read b
  fold a

# Addition is associative: (a + b) + c = a + (b + c), for every a, b, c. Proved by induction on a (each side recurses on
# a, and the successor cases align through the hypothesis). No lemma needed: the recurrence alone closes it.
rule plus-is-associative
  seat a, like natural
  seat b, like natural
  seat c, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call plus
        call plus
          read a
          read b
        read c
      call plus
        read a
        call plus
          read b
          read c
  fold a

# Addition is commutative: a + b = b + a, for every a and b. Proved by induction on a, CHAINING two previously proven
# lemmas: the base case a = 0 needs n + 0 = n (right identity), and the successor case needs a + succ b = succ (a + b)
# (the successor steps out on the right). The `cite`s name those lemmas, which the induction applies as rewrites.
rule plus-is-commutative
  seat a, like natural
  seat b, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call plus
        read a
        read b
      call plus
        read b
        read a
  fold a
    cite plus-zero-right-is-identity
    cite plus-succ-right-steps-out

# Multiplication by recursion on the first argument: 0 * b = 0, and (succ a) * b = b + (a * b). Recursion descends on
# the matched predecessor, so it is verified terminating and the kernel unfolds it.
task times
  take a, like natural
  take b, like natural
  like natural
  fork case, read a
    case zero
      send back
        make zero
    case succ
      send back
        call plus
          read b
          call times
            read prior
            read b

# Zero is a left absorber for multiplication: 0 * n = 0, for every n. Definitional.
rule times-zero-left-is-zero
  seat n, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call times
        make zero
        read n
      make zero
  calm hold

# Zero is ALSO a right absorber: n * 0 = 0, for every n. Not definitional (times recurses on its first argument), so
# proved by induction on n: the successor case 0 + (k * 0) reduces to k * 0, which the hypothesis sends to zero.
rule times-zero-right-is-zero
  seat n, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call times
        read n
        make zero
      make zero
  fold n

# One is a right identity: n * 1 = n, for every n. The successor case 1 + (k * 1) reduces to succ (k * 1), which the
# hypothesis closes. Proved by induction on n.
rule times-one-right-is-identity
  seat n, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call times
        read n
        make succ
          bind prior
            make zero
      read n
  fold n

# The successor steps out on the LEFT of a product: (succ a) * b = b + (a * b). This is the recurrence of `times`,
# definitionally true, and the lemma the ring proofs below rewrite with to expose the recursive call.
rule times-succ-left-steps-out
  seat a, like natural
  seat b, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call times
        make succ
          bind prior
            read a
        read b
      call plus
        read b
        call times
          read a
          read b
  calm hold

# Right distributivity: (a + b) * c = a*c + b*c, for every a, b, c. Proved by induction on a, CHAINING the two
# definitional step lemmas (which rewrite the recurrence into view) with associativity of addition (which realigns the
# two sides). The induction hypothesis fires automatically on the recursive call.
rule times-distributes-right
  seat a, like natural
  seat b, like natural
  seat c, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call times
        call plus
          read a
          read b
        read c
      call plus
        call times
          read a
          read c
        call times
          read b
          read c
  fold a
    cite plus-succ-steps-out
    cite times-succ-left-steps-out
    cite plus-is-associative

# Multiplication is associative: (a * b) * c = a * (b * c), for every a, b, c. Proved by induction on a, citing the
# left step lemma (to expose the recurrence) and right distributivity (to split the product over the sum it unfolds to).
rule times-is-associative
  seat a, like natural
  seat b, like natural
  seat c, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call times
        call times
          read a
          read b
        read c
      call times
        read a
        call times
          read b
          read c
  fold a
    cite times-succ-left-steps-out
    cite times-distributes-right

# The successor steps out on the RIGHT of a product: a * (succ b) = a + (a * b). Not definitional (times recurses on its
# first argument), so proved by induction on a. The step needs to commute and reassociate a sum, so the proof cites
# addition's commutativity and associativity, which the prover handles by normalizing the additive chains modulo AC.
rule times-succ-right-is-plus
  seat a, like natural
  seat b, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call times
        read a
        make succ
          bind prior
            read b
      call plus
        read a
        call times
          read a
          read b
  fold a
    cite times-succ-left-steps-out
    cite plus-succ-steps-out
    cite plus-is-associative
    cite plus-is-commutative

# Multiplication is commutative: a * b = b * a, for every a and b. Proved by induction on a: the base case needs the
# right absorber n * 0 = 0, and the successor case rewrites both sides through the two product step lemmas, the induction
# hypothesis closing k * b = b * k.
rule times-is-commutative
  seat a, like natural
  seat b, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call times
        read a
        read b
      call times
        read b
        read a
  fold a
    cite times-zero-right-is-zero
    cite times-succ-left-steps-out
    cite times-succ-right-is-plus

# A concrete product: two times two is four, computed through the kernel (multiplication unfolds into two additions).
rule two-times-two-is-four
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call times
        call two
        call two
      call plus
        call two
        call two
  calm hold

# Exponentiation by repeated multiplication: b^0 = 1, and b^(succ d) = b * b^d. Recursion descends on the exponent, so
# it is verified terminating and the kernel unfolds it. This is the reach of an associative query: in d broadcast steps
# a branching-b search reaches b^d cells (see computation/associative/search.tree).
task power
  take b, like natural
  take d, like natural
  like natural
  fork case, read d
    case zero
      send back
        make succ
          bind prior
            make zero
    case succ
      send back
        call times
          read b
          call power
            read b
            read prior

# b to the zero is one. Definitional.
rule power-zero-is-one
  seat b, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call power
        read b
        make zero
      make succ
        bind prior
          make zero
  calm hold

# the exponent step: b^(succ d) = b * b^d. Definitional (the recurrence of power).
rule power-succ-steps-out
  seat b, like natural
  seat d, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call power
        read b
        make succ
          bind prior
            read d
      call times
        read b
        call power
          read b
          read d
  calm hold

# Exponents ADD over a product: b^(d + e) = b^d * b^e, for every b, d, e. Proved by induction on d, chaining the additive
# step lemma, the multiplicative step lemma, associativity of multiplication, and (in the base) the right identity of
# addition. This is the general law behind "doubling the search depth squares the reach": b^(2d) = (b^d)^2.
rule power-adds-exponents
  seat b, like natural
  seat d, like natural
  seat e, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call power
        read b
        call plus
          read d
          read e
      call times
        call power
          read b
          read d
        call power
          read b
          read e
  fold d
    cite power-succ-steps-out
    cite power-zero-is-one
    cite plus-succ-steps-out
    cite plus-zero-left-is-identity
    cite times-succ-left-steps-out
    cite times-zero-left-is-zero
    cite times-is-associative
    cite plus-zero-right-is-identity

# Left distributivity: a * (b + c) = a*b + a*c, for every a, b, c. Proved by induction on a; the successor case spreads
# the product over the sum and the two sides agree after commuting and reassociating the additive terms (AC). With right
# distributivity this completes the distributive law of the semiring on both sides.
rule times-distributes-left
  seat a, like natural
  seat b, like natural
  seat c, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call times
        read a
        call plus
          read b
          read c
      call plus
        call times
          read a
          read b
        call times
          read a
          read c
  fold a
    cite times-succ-left-steps-out
    cite plus-is-commutative
    cite plus-is-associative

# One is a LEFT identity for multiplication: 1 * n = n, for every n. (Right identity n * 1 = n is proved above; this
# completes the multiplicative identity on both sides.) The single product step unfolds 1 * n to n + 0*n = n + 0 = n.
rule times-one-left-is-identity
  seat n, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call times
        make succ
          bind prior
            make zero
        read n
      read n
  fold n
    cite times-succ-left-steps-out
    cite times-zero-left-is-zero
    cite plus-zero-right-is-identity

# Two times n is n doubled: n * 2 = n + n, for every n. Connects multiplication by two to addition.
rule times-two-is-double
  seat n, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call times
        read n
        make succ
          bind prior
            make succ
              bind prior
                make zero
      call plus
        read n
        read n
  fold n
    cite times-succ-left-steps-out
    cite plus-succ-left-steps-out
    cite plus-succ-right-steps-out
    cite plus-zero-right-is-identity
    cite times-zero-left-is-zero

# The square of a sum: (a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2, written `(a+b)*(a+b) = a*a + (a*b + a*b) + b*b`. A classic algebraic
# identity, proved by spreading the product over the sum on both sides (both distributive laws) and commuting the cross
# term, with addition AND multiplication normalized modulo associativity-commutativity. Exercises the whole semiring.
rule square-of-sum
  seat a, like natural
  seat b, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call times
        call plus
          read a
          read b
        call plus
          read a
          read b
      call plus
        call plus
          call times
            read a
            read a
          call plus
            call times
              read a
              read b
            call times
              read a
              read b
        call times
          read b
          read b
  fold a
    cite times-distributes-right
    cite times-distributes-left
    cite times-is-commutative
    cite times-is-associative
    cite plus-is-commutative
    cite plus-is-associative
